
[38;5;231m⏺[39m CSV and JSON it is — both, no PDF. That's a bigger surface than text-only, so let me name the consequence:
  [38;5;153msummarize.py[39m grows from one renderer to three, and [38;5;153mexport[39m needs a format selector. Still bounded — the
  render-rows-then-emit flow is already here to read, and [38;5;153mcsv[39m/[38;5;153mjson[39m are stdlib so there's no new dependency. But the next
  question is the one that's genuinely hard to walk back.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what the structured files say a row [3mis[0m. [38;5;153mrender_text[39m currently produces [38;5;153macct-1: 10.50[39m plus a trailing [38;5;153mtotal:
[39m  [38;5;153m13.25[39m line — display strings, with the total mixed in among the data rows. CSV and JSON can copy that or break from
  it.

  - [1mInteger cents, total as a separate field[0m — CSV is [38;5;153maccount_id,cents[39m with [38;5;153macct-1,1050[39m and [3mno[0m total row; JSON is
    [38;5;153m{"day": ..., "rows": [{"account_id": ..., "cents": 1050}], "total_cents": 13325}[39m. Buys lossless values and a clean
    parse: every CSV line is a real account. Costs you a consumer that has to divide by 100 to display dollars.
  - [1mMirror the text output[0m — CSV is [38;5;153macct-1,10.50[39m with a [38;5;153mtotal,13.25[39m row; JSON carries the same formatted strings. Buys a
    file that reads identically to the email. Costs precision (floats/strings instead of the integer the database
    stores) and makes the CSV's last row a fake account that every parser has to special-case.

  Both are a contract the moment a downstream job reads one, so this is the expensive choice, not a formatting
  preference.

  [1mMy recommendation: integer cents with the total as a separate field.[0m [38;5;153mstore.daily_rows[39m already hands you integer cents;
  formatting is a presentation concern that belongs in [38;5;153mrender_text[39m and nowhere else. A [38;5;153mtotal[39m row in a CSV is the
  classic thing that poisons a spreadsheet import.
